points.explanation:
{すぎる} means 'to go past', so attached to a verb or adjective it means 'too much / too ~'.

- Verbs: {ます}-stem + {すぎる}: {食[た]べすぎる} 'overeat', {飲[の]みすぎる} 'drink too much', {働[はたら]きすぎる}
- {い}-adjectives: drop {い}: {高[たか]すぎる} 'too expensive', {大[おお]きすぎる}
- {な}-adjectives: stem + {すぎる}: {静[しず]かすぎる}, {簡単[かんたん]すぎる}
- Irregular: {いい} → {よすぎる}; {ない} → {なさすぎる}

{すぎる} is a {る}-verb, so it conjugates normally: {食[た]べすぎた}, {高[たか]すぎます}, {多[おお]すぎて}. The stem {すぎ} works as a noun: {飲[の]みすぎに注意[ちゅうい]} 'beware of drinking too much'.

It usually implies something negative — 'more than is good'.
